swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds analytically

Replaces the interpolation fallback used by nine house systems with closed
form derivatives, and fixes five cases where the existing speeds were wrong.

Until now Sunshine (I, i), Pullen SD (L), Pullen SR (Q), Sripati (S),
Meridian (X), Morinus (M), Carter (F), Alcabitius (B) and APC (Y) set
do_interpol and had their cusp speeds differenced against armc by two extra
CalcH() calls. That works until a cusp turns quickly, which is exactly where a
chart wants the speed: inside the polar circle the differenced value can be
wrong by hundreds of degrees per day. Each of those systems now differentiates
its own cusp formula, and drops the two extra calls with it.

Nine separate defects are fixed along the way:

  - Krusinski (U) returned exactly 0 for eight of its twelve cusp speeds.
    Only 1, 4, 7 and 10 were ever filled, by the generic code before the
    switch. The case now differentiates its chain of rotations.
  - Porphyry (O) grew cusps 11 and 12 from ac_speed. They are offsets from
    the MC and grow from mc_speed.
  - Koch (K) held the ascensional difference ad3 fixed while differentiating,
    although it moves as the MC does; eight of the twelve speeds change, by
    up to 28 per cent at latitude 60. The standalone Koch patch carries the
    derivation and the demonstration.
  - Whole sign (W, N) left ac_speed and mc_speed on cusps 1 and 10, and
    through the mirroring on 4 and 7, although those cusps stand still
    between sign ingresses. They are zero now. The sidereal path needs its
    own line for this: sidereal_houses_trad() computes 'W' as equal houses
    and imposes the sign boundaries afterwards, so case 'W' never runs and
    the equal house speeds would otherwise survive on the pinned cusps. It
    returned 600.727650 degrees per day for every cusp of a sidereal whole
    sign chart at J2000 and latitude 52.5 before this patch.
  - Placidus and Gauquelin (P, G) used AscDash() alone, which holds the pole
    height fixed. The pole height follows the cusp, so the total derivative
    needs the implicit function theorem; plac_speed() supplies it, with both
    partials in closed form.
  - The same test's site before the loops, where the first estimate is
    seeded, reported armc_speed and so dropped the obliquity factor. It is
    live: instrumented against the unpatched library it fires 53,240 times in
    5,181,120 Placidus and Gauquelin calls, about one in a hundred, and the
    value it substitutes differs from the old one by 22 per cent on average
    and by up to a factor of 8.2. It now reports AscDash() at the pole height
    that belongs to the cusp.
  - The |tant| < VERY_SMALL branch inside the iteration loops of Placidus and
    of Gauquelin sectors sets the speed and breaks, and the plac_speed() call
    after the loop then overwrote it, in all six loops that have one. The
    overwrite is worse than a wasted assignment: when that branch fires the
    cusp is set to rectasc while f still holds the pole height of an earlier
    iterate, so plac_speed() is handed a pole that does not belong to the cusp
    beside it. It is guarded now, and positions stay bit-identical with no
    measured speed changing.

    The branch appears to be unreachable rather than merely unvisited, though
    that is an argument and not a proof. Each of these loops sits inside an
    else, entered only when the same test failed on the first estimate,
    Asc1(rectasc, fh1). Asc1 carries right ascension 0 to longitude 0 and 180
    to 180 whatever the pole height, so a cusp can only reach the ecliptic
    equator, where tant vanishes, if its rectasc is already there, and then
    the outer branch has claimed it before the loop starts. Searching for a
    counterexample agrees: driving a Placidus cusp towards the equator over
    latitude, armc and obliquity, while holding its rectasc away from the
    equinoxes, stalls at |sin(cusp)| = 6.5e-3, seven orders above the 2.5e-10
    the branch needs. Unconstrained the search reaches exactly zero, at
    latitude -65, armc 210, obliquity 24, and there rectasc is 0 and the outer
    branch takes it. The guard is kept because the argument rests on a
    property of Asc1() that nothing in this file enforces.
  - Sunshine, Makransky variant (i), placed a cusp 180 degrees wrong when the
    house circle stood exactly on w = 180. Its quadrant chain tests
    w > 180 && w < 270 with a strict bound, so 180 matched none of the first
    three branches and fell through to the fourth, which returns 360 - r; r is
    exactly 0 there because tan(180) is, so the cusp came back as 360 where
    both neighbours give 180. On the equator the diurnal and nocturnal arcs are
    exactly 90 degrees and the house circles sit at exact multiples of 30, so
    every armc that is a multiple of 30 but not of 90 lands one cusp on it:
    at geolat 0 with integer armc, 8 of the 360 values return a cusp on 360
    instead of 180, while Treindl (I) returns 180 at the same input. Both
    quadrant chains now let 180 belong to the branch above, where r vanishes
    and the two agree. This is a position change, the only one in this patch,
    and it is available on its own as "fix a Sunshine (Makransky) cusp 180
    degrees wrong at w = 180" for anyone who wants it without the speeds. The
    two cannot both be applied: this patch moves that chain into
    makransky_cusp_from_w() and carries the same correction there.
  - SEFLG_RADIANS converted the cusp and ascmc positions and left the speeds
    in degrees per day, so a caller asking for radians was handed the two in
    different units, a factor of 57.3 apart. Both speed arrays are converted
    with the positions now. This is conformance rather than a change of
    contract: swephprg documents the parameters as cusp_speed /* like cusps */
    and ascmc_speed /* like ascmc */, and until now they were not like them
    under that flag. It does change what the flag returns, so a caller who had
    compensated for the old behaviour will have to stop.

At the reported singular point itself, armc 270, geolat 66.5607089, eps
23.4392911, eight letters return up to 15,551,878 degrees per day unpatched -
B F I i L Q S Y - and none of the eight exceeds 665 patched. Carter comes back
exactly 0 there, the same term-flush the APC paragraphs below describe landing
on a different letter. Two letters rise instead: Gauquelin goes 331 to 1,578
and Placidus 331 to 596, both having returned the frozen-pole value this patch
replaces, at the one locus where the cusps genuinely move arbitrarily fast.

do_interpol is kept and still honoured. It is now raised only if an analytic
speed comes back non-finite, or if a cusp position itself is non-finite, so an
unexamined input degrades to the old behaviour rather than returning garbage.
No analytic speed has been seen to come back non-finite on its own. The
position test is not idle: sunshine_solution_treindl() returns OK while
leaving a NaN in four cusps - 8, 9, 11 and 12 at one probe, the membership
shifting with the configuration - at latitudes beyond about 70 degrees
with the Sun well declined, and again in cusps 3, 5, 9 and 11 within about
1e-8 degrees of either pole with the Sun near an equinox - a separate band,
same silence - and the analytic code would otherwise report those
cusps as standing still rather than as unknown, because AscDash() decides on
d > VERY_SMALL, which a NaN fails, and returns 0. That is a defect in the
position code and is left alone here; the guard only keeps the speeds as
honest about it as the interpolation they replace was.

Positions are bit-identical for twenty five of the twenty six house systems,
over 104,523,552 cusp values across latitudes -89.5 to 89.5 and a full armc
sweep, with identical return codes and error strings.

The twenty sixth is 'i', Sunshine in the Makransky variant, and it does move.
This patch carries the same boundary correction as the standalone Makransky
patch, so a house circle landing exactly on w = 180, or exactly on w = 90 or
270 in the southern hemisphere, now returns a different cusp. On an
exact-landing sweep -- Sun declination exactly 0, armc an exact multiple of 30,
latitudes -89 to 89 -- 2,856 of 650,304 cusp values change, and the worst is
exactly 180 degrees. 'I', the Treindl variant, does not move at all.

The new value is the correct one, and that can be checked without trusting the
derivation. At armc 60, latitude -45, Sun declination 0, cusp 11:

    armc - 1e-11    78.748664951741    78.748664951741
    armc - 1e-12    78.725564536734    78.725564536734
    armc exactly   281.248688833481    78.751311166519
    armc + 1e-12    78.676778465349    78.676778465349
    armc + 1e-11    78.750826267262    78.750826267262

Both one-sided limits are near 78.75. The unpatched library returns a value 180
degrees away at the exact point and agrees everywhere either side of it, so what
this removes is an isolated discontinuity, not a shift in the surface.

Speeds were checked against a Richardson extrapolated difference of the cusp
positions, taken from a build with the Placidus iteration tightened to 1e-13
degrees so that the reference is not limited by the solver. That reference is
still limited by double precision, and below about 1e-3 degrees of armc the
cancellation in the difference dominates: the residuals quoted below are the
reference's, not the formulae's. Where a residual looked large it was rerun
over a range of step sizes and the analytic value is the one the well
conditioned steps converge on. apc_sector_speed() was checked separately in
50 digit arithmetic against the exact derivative of apc_sector() and agrees to
1e-40 at every latitude, the equator and the polar circle included; note when
repeating that check that cusp 4 sits exactly on the atan2 branch cut at
armc 0, and a reference that does not unwrap there reports a spurious 100 per
cent error. Mean relative error, worst systems first:

    U   6.7e-01 -> 7.0e-11      O   4.1e-01 -> 1.7e-11
    K   8.8e-02 -> 9.1e-12      G   3.8e-03 -> 8.1e-12
    P   3.5e-03 -> 8.0e-12      Q   7.5e-09 -> 2.0e-11

One system does not reach that level everywhere on its own. Strictly within
1e-10 degrees of the equator apc_sector() replaces the declination of the
Ascendant by a constant, and the APC cusps it returns there are not the
Ascendant and Descendant at all. That is a defect in the position code, and
repairing it
would move positions, which this patch does not do. apc_sector_speed() here
differentiates the corrected form, so applying the companion patch
"fix APC cusps near the equator" brings APC to 1e-11 at every latitude,
measured over 700000 samples. Without that patch the speeds in the band are
right and the positions they accompany are not. The band is about a fiftieth
of a millimetre wide on the ground. See the comment at case 'Y'.

The two patches are independent and may be applied in either order, producing
the same file either way; applying only one leaves a working library.

Cost, 200000 calls with speeds requested, relative to the current code. gcc
13.3.0 at -O2 with -ffp-contract=off, pinned to one core, smallest of nine
interleaved passes, latitudes drawn uniformly over the whole range. Run to run
these move by up to 8.9 per cent even pinned, so read anything within a tenth
as unchanged:

    B F I i L M Q S X   0.39x to 0.43x   two CalcH calls dropped
    Y                   0.45x to 0.67x, draw-dependent
    A C D E H J K N O R T V W   0.99x to 1.03x
    P                   1.44x            a real derivative is now computed
    G                   1.46x
    U                   1.61x

The figure depends on where you sample: weighting the draw towards the polar
regions makes P and G worse, because that is where the iteration works hardest.
The cost is also only paid by callers who ask for speeds; with cusp_speed and
ascmc_speed passed as NULL the whole set sits inside the noise band.

The systems that get cheaper do so because the interpolation they used to need
cost two extra CalcH calls, which is more than any of these derivatives cost.
Nine of them run at about four tenths of their old cost. The three that get
dearer
are the three that previously returned something cheap and wrong: U had eight
cusp speeds fixed at zero, and P and G leaned on AscDash() alone with the pole
height frozen.

plac_speed() refines its fixed point to 1e-9 degrees rather than the 1e-12 an
earlier draft used, which is a small saving; the reasoning and the measurements
are at that loop, including why a fixed step count was tried there and rejected.
U is a separate path: its cost is swe_cotrans_sp() called once per sector.

Known limitation, unchanged by this patch: APC cusp speeds are unreliable in a
small region around the configuration where the ecliptic coincides with the
horizon, that is geographic latitude on a polar circle with armc on 90 or 270
degrees. The cause is in the position code, not here. apc_sector() forms
kv = atan(T cos az / (1 + T sin az)) with T = tan(ph) tan(e), and at that
configuration T is exactly -1, so the denominator is a difference of two
quantities of size 1 that cancels away. Within about 1e-4 degrees of the
degeneracy the cusps apc_sector() returns are themselves wrong, by 222 degrees
at the worst point sampled, and a speed that accompanies a wrong cusp is wrong
with it. Verified against 50 digit arithmetic, and byte for byte identical in
the unpatched library, so it is upstream and neither patch moves it.

The interpolated speeds this patch replaces were no better there, returning
1016, 85881 and 11877236 degrees per day at three neighbouring points, and the
analytic ones reach further: the worst value measured inside the ring is
-2.5e19, on a cusp whose true rate is 72.93, where an internal numerator and
denominator cancel together and the quotient keeps only noise. Nothing catches
these, all being finite; the do_interpol fallback triggers only on a
non-finite value, and rebuilding it to catch them just hands back the
interpolation this patch exists to replace, which returns 7776165.5989,
-15551877.9797 and 15551834.4013 at that latitude and armc, matching the
unpatched library to every digit printed. At the degeneracy itself the two
one-sided limits are 331.197856 and 194.150467 degrees per day, so the
derivative does not exist there and no single value is right even in
principle.

One repair is in this patch. The denominator of dkv is written as the sum of
squares (1 + T sin az)^2 + (T cos az)^2 rather than the expanded
1 + T^2 + 2 T sin az. The two are the same number, but the expanded form is a
difference of near-equal quantities when T is near -1: it underflows to
exactly zero in a window about 1e-6 degrees of armc wide around the
coincidence, and took a guard with it that then reported all twelve cusps as
standing still. The sum of squares cannot cancel, so that guard is now
unreachable - its smallest possible value is floored by the square of the
smallest cosine a double argument can produce, 6.1e-17, and zero fires were
seen in 38.7 million evaluations across the degenerate family against 487,152
for the old form. Sampled against 60 digit arithmetic across the ring, the
rewrite takes the share of speeds within one per cent of the truth from 48.9
to 94.7 per cent and removes every exact zero the old denominator produced;
at ordinary latitudes it changes nothing that matters, median relative change
2.8e-16 and worst 6.1e-8, on a cusp moving 0.014 degrees per day. What
remains of the degeneracy after it: exact zeros can still come out of the
ring when the numerator's own two terms both flush, and the 1e19 blowups
above, both confined to the 1e-6 degree neighbourhood.

Three further repairs were tried and stay out. Substituting the limit
kv = +-90 degrees is exact for five of the twelve cusps and sends N and D
both to zero for the other seven. Stepping forward past the underflow lands
inside the unreliable ring and returns -2.4e18 on cusp 11. Rewriting
1 + T sin az as cos(ph - e) / (cos ph cos e) minus 2 T sin^2(45 - az/2) picks
the wrong branch approaching from above and disagrees with the present form
by 8e-4 relative over 5.2 million ordinary evaluations, having traded one
cancellation for another. A full repair has to reformulate kv and its
derivative together and belongs with the position code.

Completeness was checked rather than assumed. Filling the internal cusp_speed
array with a sentinel before every CalcH() call and looking for survivors finds
none: 6,572,160 cusp speeds over all twenty six house systems, latitudes -89
to 89 and a full armc sweep are each written by the case block that owns them,
so nothing reaches the caller from uninitialised stack. All five house systems
that fall back to Porphyry inside the polar circle -- G, I, i, K and P -- carry
the fallback's speeds with the fallback's cusps.

Checked at the public entry point as well as the armc one: given the same true
obliquity, swe_houses_ex2() and swe_houses_armc_ex2() return identical cusps
and identical speeds, bit for bit, across all twenty six house systems. Every
entry of ascmc_speed is the derivative of the matching entry of ascmc - to
4e-11 on this header's own sweep, and to 4.3e-08 at the worst point of the
harness's broader 25,916-date sample -
Vertex, equatorial ascendant, both co-ascendants and the polar ascendant
included, not only the Ascendant and MC. A Placidus chart inside the polar
circle reports the Porphyry speeds that go with the Porphyry cusps it falls
back to.

Known limitation, unchanged by this patch: on a polar circle, Placidus cusps 3
and 9 land on the solstice points and their speeds are wrong there, by about
13 per cent at the exact coincidence - the quantity holds one bit, so the
digit moves with the build; 12.5 on one gcc, 13.2 on another - and by as much
as 45 per cent a millionth of a degree of armc away. The damage fades by 1e-4
degrees of armc and the other ten
cusps are unaffected, agreeing with a difference of their positions to 3e-7.

The cause is not the Placidus solver. Rebuilding with VERY_SMALL_PLAC_ITER
tightened from 1/360000 to 1e-13, and niter_max raised to 1000, reproduces the
positions there bit for bit, so the fixed point is already fully converged and
the tolerance was never involved. Nor is it the degeneracy guard, which does
not fire: plac_pole_slope() reports ok at every visit.

It is cancellation in that function. The pole height responds to the cusp
through asin(v) with v = tan(fi) * tan(dek), and the slope divides by
sqrt(1 - v*v). Where a cusp sits on a solstice its declination equals the
obliquity, and on a polar circle tan(fi) is minus the cotangent of the
obliquity, so v is exactly -1 and the two conditions coincide. There
1 - v*v evaluates to 4.440892e-16, two units in the last place of 1, holding
about one bit; a cancellation-free evaluation of the same quantity gives
6.796106e-16. The 1.53 ratio becomes 1.24 through the square root and lands in
the pole term with the sign and rough size of the error observed.

Writing 1 + v as tan(fi) * (tan(dek) - tan(psi)), with psi the declination that
would send v to -1, and so as sin(dek - psi) over cosines, was tried. It moves
the analytic speed from 374.906 to 365.658 against a true 331.198: better, and
still wrong, because psi and dek are both the obliquity to within a rounding
error and the subtraction that was removed from 1 + v reappears in dek - psi. A
repair has to carry the declination as an offset from the obliquity through the
whole chain rather than reconstructing the difference at the end, which is a
change to how Placidus forms declinations and not something to bolt on here. So
nothing is changed, and the partial improvement is deliberately not shipped: it
would trade a diagnosable 13 per cent for a less obvious 10.

Known limitation, unchanged by this patch: within about 1e-5 degrees of either
pole, roughly a metre on the ground, the Alcabitius (B) cusp speeds and the
Alcabitius cusp positions disagree by a factor of 3. At latitude 89.9999989999
and armc 270 the reported speed of cusp 8 is 125.264403 degrees per day while
the returned positions move at 375.793239, stable over differencing steps from
1e-6 to 1e-3 degrees.

The cause is a degeneracy in the Ascendant, not an error in the derivative.
sda = acos(-tan(fi) * tan(dek)) is differentiated through the declination of
the Ascendant, and tan(fi) is 5.7e7 at that latitude, so d(sda)/dt comes to
about 361 degrees per day from an ac_speed of only -1.58e-5. That ac_speed is
right, matching the Ascendant's own motion over a wide window to 5e-5 relative.
But the position code's dek underflows to exactly zero in a band around armc
270, pinning sda at exactly 90 there, so the positions behave as though sda
were constant while the speeds follow the mathematics.

Which of the two is correct is not decidable from here. At the pole the
ecliptic meets the horizon at both 0 and 180 degrees of Aries, so the
Ascendant's branch is a convention rather than a fact. This file picks 0; an
independent implementation of the same formulae built for this check picks 180
and gives a third answer, 626.3 degrees per day, differing only in the sign of
cos(ac) inside d(sda)/dt. The pre-existing interpolated speeds are equally
inconsistent with the positions at most of these latitudes. Settling it means
fixing the Ascendant's branch at the poles in the position code first, so
nothing is changed here.

Two further points the sweep flagged, Pullen SR (Q) near the poles and Sunshine
(i) inside the polar circle, are artefacts of the differencing reference rather
than defects. Both converge onto the analytic value once the step is chosen to
suit the local curvature: Q reaches -169526.0737 against an analytic
-169526.0700 at a step of 1e-7, its speed being large enough that a 1e-3 step
carries real truncation error, and i reaches 150.929743103 against an analytic
150.929743087 at a step of 1e-1.

Known limitation, unchanged by this patch: the Sunshine cusp speeds, I and i,
treat the Sun as standing still. The cusps are built from the Sun's
declination, which moves, and neither the interpolated speeds nor the
closed-form ones carry that motion. The residual against a differencing
reference tracks the Sun's declination rate. Over 4,000 dates between 1800 and
1910 at random latitudes, 5,130 cusp comparisons passed the reference's own
convergence test and were judged; binned by the Sun's declination rate:

  |d(decl)/dt|, deg/day    judged     mean residual, deg/day
    0.00 to 0.08             630            5.20e-03
    0.08 to 0.16             629            1.46e-02
    0.16 to 0.24             797            2.38e-02
    0.24 to 0.32             944            2.67e-02
    0.32 to 0.40            2130            3.12e-02

Six times as much at an equinox as at a solstice, which is the shape of the
Sun's own declination rate rather than of anything in the house code. The
unpatched library shows the same residual to four digits, 1.416e-03 relative
in both, so this patch neither introduces it nor removes it. A separate
300-date sweep at random latitudes, ingress jumps excluded, puts the systems
the patch does change in context: worst relative error O 7.750e-01 before and
8.200e-06 after, P 4.156e-02 before and 8.359e-06 after, K 3.854e-01 before
and 7.240e-05 after.

Carrying the term would mean threading the Sun's declination rate - which
swe_houses_ex2() already computes with SEFLG_SPEED and discards - into the
cusp derivative. That changes what
the Sunshine code computes rather than how it is differentiated, and it buys a
system whose speeds were already among the better ones, so it is left alone.

Known limitation, unchanged by this patch: the sidereal entry points subtract
the ayanamsa from the cusp positions but not its rate from the speeds, so a
sidereal call returns the same cusp speeds as a tropical one. Confirmed
through swe_houses_ex2() with SEFLG_SIDEREAL. The term is the rate of the
ayanamsa, 0.1448 arc seconds per day for Fagan/Bradley at J2000, which is
4.0e-5 degrees. Measured as the difference between the
tropical and sidereal cusp 1 speeds at latitude 52.5, 600 43 39.5410 against
600 43 39.3962. The companion patch "subtract the ayanamsa's rate from the
sidereal cusp speeds" corrects it for sidereal_houses_trad(), the path taken
unless SE_SIDBIT_ECL_T0 or SE_SIDBIT_SSY_PLANE is asked for; the other two keep
the limitation. That patch and this one are independent and this paragraph is
true of this patch alone.
---
 swehouse.c | 1148 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------------
 1 file changed, 1031 insertions(+), 117 deletions(-)
diff --git a/swehouse.c b/swehouse.c
index 1c5bbd3..0781bb1 100644
--- a/swehouse.c
+++ b/swehouse.c
@@ -72,6 +72,118 @@ house and (simple) aspect calculation
 static double Asc1(double, double, double, double);
 static double AscDash(double, double, double, double);
 static double Asc2(double, double, double, double);
+/* VERY_SMALL is an angular tolerance in degrees, and it is the wrong thing to
+ * compare a denominator against. Sums of squares, products of cosines and the
+ * like reach 1e-10 at perfectly ordinary configurations without the cusp being
+ * degenerate, and refusing there reports a moving cusp as standing still. A
+ * denominator is only unusable when dividing by it would overflow, so the
+ * derivative code below tests against this instead. Guards that protect the
+ * domain of asin() or sqrt() keep VERY_SMALL, where a real tolerance belongs. */
+#define VERY_SMALL_DENOM 1e-200
+
+/* Units used by the cusp speed code below.
+ * Every cusp_speed[] and *_speed field is degrees of ecliptic longitude per
+ * day, and ARMCS, the rate of armc, is the only place a per-day factor enters.
+ * The helpers keep to one of two shapes:
+ *   - AscDash(), apc_sector_speed() and plac_speed() already carry the ARMCS
+ *     factor and return degrees per day directly. Note that AscDashPole(),
+ *     despite the name, belongs to the other group: it is the partial of
+ *     Asc1() in the pole height and carries no ARMCS;
+ *   - pullen_sr_slopes(), makransky_slope_w(), AscDashPole() and
+ *     plac_pole_slope() return a dimensionless degrees per degree, which is
+ *     multiplied by ARMCS, or by an existing per-day speed, exactly once at
+ *     the point of use. Case 'U' belongs to neither group: swe_cotrans_sp()
+ *     is handed rates that already carry ARMCS, so it returns degrees per day
+ *     throughout and no factor is applied at the end.
+ * Angles are in degrees throughout except inside apc_sector_speed(), which
+ * follows apc_sector() in taking radians; its result is a ratio of radians to
+ * radians, hence dimensionless, and is scaled by ARMCS like the rest. */
+
+/* Where each house system's cusp speeds come from.
+ *
+ *   A E D V     every cusp moves with the Ascendant or with the MC
+ *   C R T H     AscDash(), closed form, unchanged by this patch
+ *   P G         plac_speed(): AscDash() corrected by the implicit function
+ *               theorem, because the pole height follows the cusp
+ *   K           AscDash() times the rate of its own first argument; ad3 is
+ *               differentiated through the MC
+ *   O L S       chain rule from ac_speed and mc_speed through the quadrant
+ *               size acmc
+ *   Q           the same, but through the sector ratio r, which is
+ *               differentiated implicitly from the quadrant identity
+ *   X M         closed form through the ecliptic/equator conversion; armc is
+ *               the only thing that moves. The two use different relations and
+ *               so have different formulae, see the comments there
+ *   F           ac_speed scaled by the ratio of the two conversion factors,
+ *               the cos(eps) cancelling
+ *   B           AscDash() times the rate of its first argument; the semi-
+ *               diurnal arc is differentiated through the Ascendant
+ *   U           the rotations are carried by swe_cotrans_sp(), which moves a
+ *               velocity with its position; the conversions are closed form
+ *   Y           apc_sector() differentiated in closed form; cusps 1 4 7 10 are
+ *               not apc_sector()'s and take the Ascendant's and MC's speeds.
+ *               Opposite cusps are not 180 degrees apart in this system, so
+ *               all twelve are computed and the mirroring is skipped
+ *   I i         treindl: AscDash() is exact, the pole height does not move.
+ *               makransky: closed form, with cos(m) and sin(m) taken from
+ *               sqrt(cos^2 w + tan^2 pole) rather than from m.
+ *               As with Y, opposite cusps are not 180 degrees apart
+ *   W N         zero. The cusps stand still between sign ingresses and jump
